# 21. How Python actually runs

This is particularly interesting when comparing Python with Java.

A simplified picture of CPython is:

```text
Python source
     │
     │
     ▼
  Parser
     │
     ▼
Python bytecode
     │
     ▼
Python Virtual Machine
     │
     ▼
Machine instructions
```

For example:

```python
print("Hello")
```

is not normally compiled directly into native machine code in the same way a C program is.

CPython first compiles Python source into **bytecode**, which is then executed by the Python runtime.

This is somewhat analogous to Java:

```text
Java:

.java
  ↓
javac
  ↓
.class bytecode
  ↓
JVM
  ↓
machine code
```

Python:

```text
.py
  ↓
Python compiler
  ↓
bytecode
  ↓
Python runtime
  ↓
machine code
```

However, the implementations and runtime architectures are different.

---

# 22. Python's relationship with C

There is an important historical and technical connection here.

The most widely used Python implementation, **CPython**, is written primarily in C.

This creates a useful architecture:

```text
        Python program
              │
              ▼
        Python runtime
          (CPython)
              │
       ┌──────┴──────┐
       ▼             ▼
      C code      external libraries
                      │
                 C/C++/CUDA/etc.
```

This is one reason Python can provide a very easy programming interface while still accessing high-performance native code.

For example, NumPy's heavy numerical operations are not simply implemented as slow Python loops.

